this South African Pangolin is nicknamed  bumper he was confiscated during an  undercover operation and is one of many  brought to vet Debbie English so a lot  of people first of all don't know they  exist and they don't really understand  how endangered these little animals are  by the time they get to this Clinic the  pangolins are in desperate need of  medical treatment more often than not  they are emaciated dehydrated and most  of them have pneumonia when we when we  receive them the animals are kept in  appalling conditions before they're  traded on the black market they're left  in car boots bags and plastic drums for  days or even weeks  one Pangolin can fetch up to 15 000 US  Dollars their meat and scales which are  made of keratin are used in traditional  African and Asian medicine it's the most  traded of the illegal wildlife animals  currently and that's where it's taken  over from the Rhinos in a big way this  undercover agent for the African  Pangolin working group poses as a buyer  and once we've put the deal together  we'll set up a time and location where  we do the transaction and this is where  the sting operation will go down  Wildlife traffickers face up to 10 years  in prison in South Africa the pangolins  are confiscated and taken to a  rehabilitation center at a secret  location it takes a long time medically  to get them back into a position where  we can release them so some animals are  here for a month and then some nearly a  year it's a labor-intensive process the  challenge with rehabilitating these  animals is not just the enormous cost  that's involved but it's also the time  alignments required South African  pangolins won't eat in captivity and so  they need to forage for food for up to  six hours a day Emma Diaga has a team of  staff and volunteers and while getting  the back into the wild is the ultimate  goal conservationists say it's not  enough in every one that we save and the  time that we save that hundreds and  thousands of being poached in that time  we're not talking a matter of 10 years  they'll be extinct we're talking a year  they are shy and secretive they don't  hurt anybody but yet we're destroying  them the clinic receives no Government  funding the undercover agent also works  for free it's for each and every person  every citizen every person to actually  protect the wildlife because we are  protecting it not for ourselves but for  our future Generations  as for bumper he's been released back  into the wild one of the few success  stories in this campaign to save the  Pangolin Emily anguin Al Jazeera tzaneen  South Africa
 

